\viewkind4\pard\cf1\fs32\b Read adapters, routes and neighbours\b0\fs22\par\par
\pard\sa120 Start with the adapter: check whether it is operational and has a usable address and prefix. Then inspect the route to the actual destination. The first adapter or first default route in a list is not necessarily the path Windows will choose.\par
\pard\li240\ri240\sa120\f1 Local address: 192.168.10.42/26\par
Local prefix: 192.168.10.0/26\par
192.168.10.50 is inside that prefix.\par
192.168.10.100 is outside it and needs another applicable route.\f0\par
\pard\sa120 Route selection also depends on routes, metrics, source and interface context. Use Route to target to ask Windows about a particular destination. A routing snapshot describes current configuration; it does not prove that the next hop will answer.\par
\pard\sa120 The neighbour cache maps locally relevant IP addresses to link-layer information and state. An incomplete or stale entry is not proof that a device is permanently absent. The cache is not a complete network inventory.\par
\pard\sa120 The proposed interface, route and neighbour views are read-only. Refresh does not change routes, clear caches or scan the network.\par
